                    {
                        "sense_id": "180ed3f3-c210-3aee-80bd-994f33e18fc2",
                        "entry_id": "437c3a10-f922-3c7e-b5ab-415ad15db966",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Mot qui a un sens opposé à un autre uniquement dans certains contextes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fc58cf08-14dd-4ee0-91fd-a4593db70aa2",
                                "sense_id": "180ed3f3-c210-3aee-80bd-994f33e18fc2",
                                "text": "les antipodaux sont formés de deux termes représentant chacun les extrémités de l’axe sur lequel ils s'articulent : haut/bas pour la verticalité, début/fin pour la dimension temporelle. On peut retrouver ces oppositions élémentaires dans des paires de quasi-antonymes comme cave/grenier, tête/pied ou berceau/tombe."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "52f6bfe8-03e7-4d7a-aa11-7537517b23aa",
                        "source_entry_id": "437c3a10-f922-3c7e-b5ab-415ad15db966",
                        "source_sense_id": "180ed3f3-c210-3aee-80bd-994f33e18fc2",
                        "target_lang_word": "near-antonym",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "near-antonym",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7abb2e6c-586c-3ff2-b317-3239acfcdf1b",
                                        "entry_id": "8b59fe4e-9874-32c8-bb8f-c0622eb4d5f4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A word that is nearly an antonym, when an antonym is defined strictly by complete negation or polar oppositeness. (Many reference works use a looser definition of antonym that lumps these words into the single category of antonyms; some make the more rigorous distinction.)"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "320d1c79-49c0-4087-aba9-b219d1aa8be1",
                                                "sense_id": "7abb2e6c-586c-3ff2-b317-3239acfcdf1b",
                                                "text": "[…] Near-antonyms may be treated in a similar fashion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fbea6c4a-fe7b-4b79-8c5b-94086078da7d",
                                                "sense_id": "7abb2e6c-586c-3ff2-b317-3239acfcdf1b",
                                                "text": "Among the most common antonyms or near antonyms are the following. Make sure that you know their meanings."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d7ca9b85-2007-45db-a4a1-08bfd398b53b",
                                                "sense_id": "7abb2e6c-586c-3ff2-b317-3239acfcdf1b",
                                                "text": "[…] near antonyms (sinking/swelling; vast/faint) gives way to a set of near synonyms (weird/appealing; suggestive/wild); what starts out as opposition proves complementary instead. And all of the adjectives that testify […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d7619843-077d-4ec1-81f0-0adbe71601af",
                                                "sense_id": "7abb2e6c-586c-3ff2-b317-3239acfcdf1b",
                                                "text": "This is a revised edition of a thesaurus first published in 1980 (BRD 1981). The work uses a dictionary format, with words and numbered definitions on the left column of a page, and corresponding numbered synonyms, near-synonyms, antonyms, and near-antonyms on the right column."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ebd13a4a-4521-4b8c-b74e-d5e2c953fd6e",
                                                "sense_id": "7abb2e6c-586c-3ff2-b317-3239acfcdf1b",
                                                "text": "As a basic editing tool, Legacy has most of the usual features characteristic of Windows products. It will do columnar cut and paste, perform search and replace effectively, and maintain capitalization […] complete thesaurus with synonyms, near-synonyms, antonyms, near-antonyms, definitions, parts of speech, and references to alternative words. The speller catches double words, capitalization errors, and simple punctuation errors, such as double periods. The program does not have user dictionaries. However, it will support small exception lists — NBI suggests the lists be 50 words or fewer — for proper names and other special terms."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b2bb710c-aaf2-46c0-a469-ab6dab48b149",
                                                "sense_id": "7abb2e6c-586c-3ff2-b317-3239acfcdf1b",
                                                "text": "An antonym is a word whose meaning is directly opposite to another word's meaning. […] Words that are only opposite in some aspect of their meaning cannot be said to be true antonyms. For example, sad […] funny […] Near antonyms are words that do not qualify as antonyms under the strict definition used for this thesaurus but which are clearly in marked contrast with the members of a synonym group."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
